WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.340
Sell with confidence, handle objections, and keep a sales book.

2
00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:09.060
This is the video for Module 3 of Introduction to Pricing and Selling.

3
00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:17.440
A good price only becomes money when someone buys. Selling is a short, friendly conversation in five stages.

4
00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:23.270
First, greet warmly. Use her name, put the phone down, and on WhatsApp reply quickly.

5
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.850
Second, ask before you tell. Who is it for? When does she need it? What matters most to her?

6
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:38.890
Third, show benefits. A feature is a fact. A benefit is what that fact does for her. Link them with the words, which means.

7
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.210
Fried fresh on the morning of delivery, which means it is crunchy when your guests eat it.

8
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:52.630
Fourth, state the price plainly, with one benefit next to it. Do not apologise, and do not say you can reduce.

9
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.300
Fifth, ask for the sale. How many packs would you like? Shall I deliver on Thursday or Friday?

10
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.890
Most lost sales skip stage 2 or stage 5.

11
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:13.830
Here is how it sounds. Ngozi greets Funmi and asks who it is for. Funmi says it is a birthday party of about 25 children on Saturday.

12
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:28.730
Ngozi suggests 200 g packs, one sealed pack per child, fried fresh on Friday. The price is 800 naira a pack, so 20,000 naira for 25 packs, delivered on Saturday morning.

13
00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:35.380
Then she asks for the sale, with a 50% deposit of 10,000 naira to confirm.

14
00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:41.510
Funmi pays. Ngozi checks her bank app, sees the money, and sends a written confirmation.

15
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:49.880
An objection is a reason a customer gives for not buying yet. It is normal, and it shows she is still interested.

16
00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:54.200
First, listen to the whole objection without defending yourself.

17
00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:00.170
Second, acknowledge it. I understand, you want to be sure it is worth the money.

18
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.090
Third, ask. Is it the total amount, or are you comparing with another seller?

19
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:14.660
Fourth, respond with value, a smaller size, or honest facts. Notice that none of these starts with a price cut.

20
00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:20.170
In many markets buyers expect to bargain, so plan before you start.

21
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:32.090
Amaka decides an asking price of 18,000 naira, a target of 17,000 naira, and a walk-away price of 15,000 naira, which is above her cost.

22
00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:42.810
The customer offers 12,000 naira. Amaka asks when she needs it, and explains she can deliver in time for the wedding, with a free adjustment.

23
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:48.920
She agrees at 17,000 naira, paid in full today. Her profit is 6,000 naira.

24
00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:58.040
Sell honestly. Describe your product truthfully, give the full quantity you advertise, and be honest about delivery time.

25
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:10.960
Do not make claims you cannot prove. Do not meet in the middle each time, or you will end far below your plan. Move in small steps, and only for something in return, like full payment.

26
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.620
If a buyer only wants a price below your cost, wish her well.

27
00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:24.270
Records turn guesses into facts. Write each sale: date, item, quantity, price, sales amount, cost, profit and how she paid.

28
00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:35.470
On Monday, Ngozi sold 12 packs at 800 naira, 30 bulk packs at 750 naira, and 8 small packs at 450 naira.

29
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:47.470
Her sales were 35,700 naira and her profit was 11,100 naira. That is a margin of 31%, just as she planned.

30
00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.850
Check your cash and your app against the book the same day.

31
00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:59.970
Each week, write five numbers: total sales, gross profit, margin, discounts given, and your best and slowest items.

32
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:06.800
Sales can rise while profit falls. A falling margin is your early warning to recalculate costs.

33
00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:18.590
To remember. Follow the five stages and ask for the sale. Answer objections calmly before you ever cut a price. Plan your three prices. Write every sale.

34
00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:24.040
And to see if a fixed cost like rent is worth it, divide it by your profit per item.

35
00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:37.070
Now write your own five-stage script and practise your price sentence out loud ten times. Prepare answers to your three most common objections, and keep your sales book for 7 days.

36
00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.360
Continue with the lessons below.
